hi i'm mike coleman a developer advocate  here at google cloud  in this video i want to walk you through  how you can use google cloud's migrate  for anthos  to move existing linux workloads from  virtual machines to containers in just a  few steps  this video builds on an earlier one  introducing migrate paranthos  if you haven't watched that video yet  definitely go check it out  as this one assumes you perform the  prerequisites we outlined there  building on what you learned in the  first video let's jump into an actual  migration  as a brief recap migrate for anthos has  three primary components  the discovery tool assesses how  successful a migration of a given  application might be  the processing cluster is a kubernetes  cluster that is used to examine the  source vm  and create the migration plan as well as  execute the actual migration  and finally mig ctl a command line tool  like coop ctl  for interacting with the processing  cluster we'll do most of our work today  in the google cloud console  but i will show you a bit of mix etl and  again to quickly recap  a migration generally consists of the  following steps  first create a google cloud project set  up the necessary service accounts  and enable the appropriate apis then you  create a migration processing cluster  after that choose and qualify your  target workload  then you define your migration source  create a migration plan  and adjust that plan to ensure it meets  your needs  after that you create the actual  deployment artifacts  and then finally you can deploy the  newly containerized application  the first two steps were covered in the  previous video so we'll start by  discussing  choosing the right workload there are  some applications that are well suited  for migration  and some that are not linux applications  that are a good fit for migration using  migrate for anthos include the following  application architectures  web and application servers business  logic middleware for example  tomcat multivm multi-tier stacks for  example  lamp small and medium-sized databases  like mysql and postgres  in addition applications best suited for  migration with migrate for anthos have  the following operational  characteristics  they have a low duty cycle and bursty  workloads  they're always on with low load services  or deployment  testing and training lab environments in  general  most linux workloads are compatible with  migration however there are some types  of workloads that are not really  recommended  these would include high performance and  large in-memory databases  vms with special kernel drivers for  example kernel mode  nfs apps with dependencies on specific  hardware  and software with licenses tied to  certain hardware id registration  for this demo i'm using a java  application housed in a vm which also  has  a postgres database so i have a good  feeling that this application is an  appropriate candidate to migrate but how  can i be sure  well we have a utility for that the  migrate for anthos discovery tool  the discovery tool has two parts the  first is a collection script that runs  on the source vm  and analyzes the running application  the analysis script then looks at that  information gathered from the collection  phase  and gives us a score of 1 to 10 on the  likelihood of a successful migration  you can run your analysis script on a  centralized server allowing you to batch  process  collected data so let's jump into our  demo and look at the discovery tool  which i've already installed on the  source vm running on google compute  engine  you can consult the migrate franco  stocks for info on how to install the  tool  before we look at the discovery tool i  wanted to take a quick overview  of the sample application we're working  with which is the symbol bank app  so i'm going to go ahead and come down  here and click sign in  and what you're going to see here is a  typical banking application we're  looking at the ledger view here  i can come up and i can click send  payment i can choose  another user from the drop down we'll  use alice here i'm going to send her  a hundred dollars and we're going to  click send and you can see the ledger  update  so everything is working the way we  expect it to let's look a little bit how  that application is deployed on the back  end  the application is actually made up of a  combination of kubernetes services  and then a monolithic service running in  a virtual machine so  on the kubernetes side we have four  services running they handle everything  from managing the accounts database  uh to the the web front end we just  interacted with to that drop down list  that we saw  where we can pull up associated accounts  that the main user has  we come into here this is our vm that  we're going to migrate and that's the  ledger service so everything you saw  there that dealt with the ledger  that is handled here so how do we update  the ledger uh check balances those sort  of things the other thing is that this  monolith  includes the database the ledger  database so there's an accounts database  and a ledger database and the ledger  database  lives inside of this vm so when we're  done we're going to have migrated this  vm  into a kubernetes service so let's get  started with that  so here i am i've ssh into that ledger  monolith service vm  and as i mentioned before i've already  installed the my great franthos  discovery tool  so what we want to do here is let's take  a look at what that's comprised of you  have the collect script and the analysis  script  so we're going to go ahead and run that  collect script first so let me run that  and that's going to produce a tar file  here and we're going to go ahead  and then run the analysis script against  that tar file  so we'll go ahead and run that  and then that gives us a csv file let's  cat out the  contents of that file  so this is a little hard to read on the  screen so what i've done is i've  actually taken  and moved that csv onto my local machine  and loaded it up into a google sheet  there's a lot of information here i'm  only going to point out a couple of  things  first our fit score is a 9 and that is  right here  so we've got a 9 out of 10 and that  means it's a great fit  with none to very little manual work  based on the glossary here  the other thing i want to point out is  that we do have a postgres database  in the vm that the fit tool discovered  and we'll have to deal with that during  our migration  but with a score of nine we should feel  pretty good that we should be able to  migrate this application  with very little effort so the next step  is to actually create a migration source  so we're gonna do that by moving back  here  in the cloud console and we're gonna go  to anthos  migrate to containers remember we've  already set up our processing cluster we  did that in our last  video so the processing cluster is right  here so now we're going to create a  source  so we'll come in and we'll say add  source and we're going to select our  processing cluster  click next we're going to go ahead and  give this source a name  and it's going to come from compute  engine remember it can come from vmware  or  aws or azure as well but in this case  we're using compute engine  we'll click next as in the first video  we  created one service account to actually  let us install the components on the  processing cluster  and now we're going to create a second  service account that lets us deal with  that compute engine as a source so we  will  put the compute viewer and the compute  storage admin roles onto this service  account  we'll click next and we'll go ahead and  add the source  so we come back here we have our source  all ready for us to go  next let's move on and create our  migration now one thing to note is that  before i can actually migrate a vm i  have to stop it which i've already done  i've gone into the  google cloud console and i've stopped  that ledger service vm so it's not  running any longer  with that i would come in here now and i  click create migration  i take the migration name and i'll give  it a name here  we'll come down we'll select the source  that we just created  linux operating system and the virtual  machine id  is ledger monolith dash  service so right there and we're going  to migrate both the  image and data in this case that  monolith vm has postgres running in it  so we need both the  application code and the data so let's  click create migration  this will take a couple minutes so i'll  fast forward the video and i'll rejoin  you when it's done  our migration plan has been generated so  the next step here is now to make sure  that migration plan is accurate for  what we want to do so we can come here  to options and we can review and edit  the migration plan  now i know one thing i'm going to need  to change is  down here under data volumes i need to  go ahead and tell it that i want to use  the postgresql folder right so we're  going to point it at that postgres  subdirectory because remember we have  that database running  there in our monolithic vm so with that  i can save this  and generate my artifacts so let's click  save and generate artifacts  and it will save it and it'll start  generating the artifacts again  this will take a couple of minutes so  i'll pause the video and i'll rejoin you  when it finishes  so the migration is completed and our  artifacts have been generated i can  click on the link of the migration here  and click on the artifacts tab and you  can get a list of all of them so we have  a docker file but  the docker image has actually already  been created and uploaded to container  registry for us so we don't need to do  anything with that  what we really care about is this  deployment spec now there's a couple  ways you can grab the deployment spec  or any of the artifacts for that matter  one is they're stored in  a storage bucket up on google cloud  storage  and you could download them from here  the other way to do it is to use mig ctl  the command line utility and that's what  we're gonna do  so i'm gonna move over into cloud shell  and i'm gonna issue a mig  ctl migration get artifacts command  and that's gonna download the artifacts  for me  so let me go ahead here and i'll list  out the deployment spec.yaml just to  verify it got downloaded  and that's a standard kubernetes yaml  file for deploying the service and some  other stuff  we're not going to spend a lot of time  going into that but what we are going to  do now is actually apply it  now the processing cluster is different  from the production cluster where i run  my applications so i need to actually  authenticate into that production  cluster so let me go ahead and  issue the gcloud command to authenticate  into my production cluster  and we will list out the nodes just to  make sure i have the right cluster  and i do so the next step is to actually  just do  a kubectl minus f apply  and apply the deployment spec  and that's going to create a stateful  set it's going to create a service  a persistent volume and a persistent  volume claim the persistent volume and  the persistent volume claim will be used  to handle that postgres database we  migrated over from our monolith vm  the next thing we need to do is ensure  all of the services can find each other  the symbol bank app does this through a  set of configuration variables  currently the variables associated with  the ledger service are pointing to the  compute engine  internal dns name of the ledger monolith  vm  which has the format of instance name  dot c  dot project id dot internal which you  can see right here  i'm going to swap those out for the  kubernetes service name  which is simply ledger monolith service  and if i list my kubernetes services you  can see that service listed right here  so the next thing to do is actually  apply that config map so those new  service  mappings take place so we'll apply the  config map here  next let's go ahead and delete all the  existing pods  and that will force them to pick up the  new mappings when they  regenerate and after that what we're  going to do  is we're going to list our services so  we can grab the ip address  of our front end and check out and make  sure the application's still working as  expected so i'll copy this  and i'm going to paste it up here into  my browser so the symbol bank the app  has loaded let's sign in again  so we're signed in here's the ledger  let's go into a send payment to alice  again let's give her 250  this time and we'll click send  and we can see the ledger is updated so  our application is working exactly as we  expected to after a successful migration  so there you have it in this video you  learned about using migrate for anthos  to move linux workloads into containers  including how to assess your application  create the migration plan  execute the migration and deploy your  newly containerized application  be sure to like and subscribe because  we'll be posting subsequent videos in  this series  to cover topics such as best practices  for evaluating your application  how to migrate windows workloads and  more to get started with migrate4anthos  today visit  slash cloud.google.com slash anthos  [Music]  you

